</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhizhong Zhao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ke Chen
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>The authors are with the Department of Computer Science, University of Manchester, Manchester M13 9PL, U.K. (e-mail: zhizhong.zhao@postgrad.manchester.ac.uk; ke.chen@manchester.ac.uk).
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Uncertainty quantification (UQ) is vital for trustworthy deep learning, yet existing methods are either computationally intensive, such as Bayesian or ensemble methods, or provide only partial, task-specific estimates, such as single-forward-pass techniques.
In this paper, we propose a post-hoc single-forward-pass framework that jointly captures aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty without modifying or retraining pretrained models.
Our method applies <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Split-Point Analysis</em> (SPA) to decompose predictive residuals into upper and lower subsets, computing <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mean Absolute Residuals</em> (MARs) on each side. We prove that, under ideal conditions, the total MAR equals the harmonic mean of subset MARs; deviations define a novel <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Self-consistency Discrepancy Score</em> (SDS) for fine-grained epistemic estimation across regression and classification.
For regression, side-specific quantile regression yields prediction intervals with improved empirical coverage, which are further calibrated via SDS.
For classification, when calibration data are available, we apply SPA-based calibration identities to adjust the softmax outputs and then compute predictive entropy on these calibrated probabilities.
Extensive experiments on diverse regression and classification benchmarks demonstrate that our framework matches or exceeds several state-of-the-art UQ methods while incurring minimal overhead.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Uncertainty quantification, split-point self-consistency, aleatoric-epistemic disentanglement, calibration, trustworthy deep learning

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uncertainty quantification (UQ) in machine learning (ML) aims to quantify uncertainties associated with model predictions, typically distinguishing between <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">aleatoric</span> (data) uncertainty, which stems from intrinsic data variability, and <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">epistemic</span> (model) uncertainty, which arises from limitations in the model itself <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. UQ is not only critical for improving the reliability and interpretability of ML models but also indispensable in safety-critical applications such as autonomous driving and AI-based medical diagnostics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Numerous UQ methods have been proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Bayesian inference and ensemble approaches yield high quality uncertainty estimates, but their use in deep learning (DL) is hindered by substantial computational cost. Conformal prediction offers robust guarantees, yet it requires an exchangeable calibration set and does not distinguish aleatoric from epistemic uncertainty. Accordingly, recent efforts have shifted towards efficient single-forward-pass UQ methods for DL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Despite their efficiency, these techniques suffer from four main limitations:
(i) reliance on explicit distributional assumptions, causing misaligned calibration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>;
(ii) imprecise epistemic estimates that function more as <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">out-of-distribution</span> (OOD) detectors than fine-grained uncertainty measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>;
(iii) separate estimation of aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty in regression, leading to misaligned <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predictive intervals</span> (PIs) and calibration errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>; and
(iv) with the sole exception of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, no unified method quantifies both uncertainty types, supports diverse ML tasks, and integrates seamlessly with an already deployed DL model (hereinafter termed the <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">base model</em>) without modifying its architecture or retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose a unified UQ framework that directly addresses these limitations, as illustrated in Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To address limitation (i), our method avoids any distributional assumptions and, leveraging an existing DL base model, quantifies both aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty in a single forward pass via <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">split-point analysis</em> (SPA). In SPA, predictive residuals are partitioned around the point-prediction into upper and lower subsets, and the corresponding split-point <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mean absolute residuals</em> (MARs) are estimated independently. Under heteroscedastic conditions, we prove that, for a perfect model, the total MAR equals the harmonic mean of its subset MARs; this self-consistency constraint forms our theoretical foundation. For an imperfect model, deviations from the harmonic identity then yield a fine-grained measure of epistemic uncertainty for both regression and classification, hence improving upon coarse OOD-style detectors and addressing limitation (ii).
In regression, our method jointly applies split-point quantile regression (QR) to the upper and lower subsets, producing PIs and estimating MARs, thereby addressing limitation (iii). Unlike simultaneous QR on the full dataset in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, our split-point QR achieves improved empirical PI coverage; these intervals can then be calibrated via MAR-based self-consistency verification on the original training data to incorporate epistemic uncertainty without extra calibration sets. When calibration data are available for classification, we combine predictive-distribution entropy for aleatoric uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> with SPA-based calibration identities derived from zero-included MARs to correct base model over- or under-confidence. Finally, our framework addresses limitation (iv) by operating post-hoc and model-agnostically on already deployed DL base models, as depicted in Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a single-pass unified UQ framework that quantifies both aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty, supports regression and classification, and seamlessly integrates with deployed DL models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide rigorous theoretical underpinnings for the SPA-based self-consistency principle, establishing its validity for efficient and reliable UQ.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The self-consistency principle enables joint uncertainty modeling without any distributional assumptions, post-hoc calibration of PIs in regression without extra calibration sets, and confidence correction in classification when calibration data are available.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive evaluations on diverse benchmark and real-world datasets, demonstrating that our method is competitive with or outperforms several state-of-the-art UQ methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13262v1/model_structure.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/126;" width="429" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Our unified UQ framework, based on the split-point analysis and the self-consistency principle (Section <a href="#S3" title="III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>), operates as follows. <span id="S1.F1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In the left panel (Regression)</span>, we leverage the <span id="S1.F1.28" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;">base model</span> and employ an <span id="S1.F1.29" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">MLP</span> regressor to jointly learn <span id="S1.F1.30" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">split-point QR</span> (<math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{QR}^{+},\mathrm{QR}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">QR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">QR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{QR}^{+},\mathrm{QR}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the three <span id="S1.F1.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">MARs</span> (<math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR},\mathrm{MAR}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">MAR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">MAR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">+</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">MAR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;" mathcolor="#F9C499">−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR},\mathrm{MAR}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) on the <span id="S1.F1.32" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">training set</span> (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A Architecture and Learning for Regression ‣ V UQ Network Architecture and Learning ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>). For <span id="S1.F1.33" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">test data</span>, the trained MLP produces MAR estimates via its three MAR heads for self-consistency verification to quantify <span id="S1.F1.34" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">epistemic uncertainty</span> (Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="VI-A Quantifying Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-A</span></a>); the split-point QR heads, together with the base model’s <span id="S1.F1.35" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;">point prediction</span>, yield <span id="S1.F1.36" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">PIs</span> for <span id="S1.F1.37" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">aleatoric uncertainty</span>, which are further calibrated by self-consistency verification (Section <a href="#S6.SS2.SSS1" title="VI-B1 Aleatoric Uncertainty in Regression ‣ VI-B Quantifying Aleatoric Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-B1</span></a>).
<span id="S1.F1.38" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In the right panel (Classification)</span>, we leverage the <span id="S1.F1.39" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;">base model</span> and employ an <span id="S1.F1.40" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">MLP</span> to learn the total <span id="S1.F1.41" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">MAR</span>
on the <span id="S1.F1.42" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">training set</span> (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Architecture and Learning for Classification ‣ V UQ Network Architecture and Learning ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>).
For <span id="S1.F1.43" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">test data</span>, the trained MLP’s total <span id="S1.F1.44" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#F9C499;">MAR</span> estimate and two base-model derived <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;" mathcolor="#92CDDC">MAR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;" mathcolor="#92CDDC">+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.F1.45" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;">, <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;" mathcolor="#92CDDC">MAR</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#92CDDC;" mathcolor="#92CDDC">−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></span> are used together in self-consistency verification to quantify <span id="S1.F1.46" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">epistemic uncertainty</span> (Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="VI-A Quantifying Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-A</span></a>).
When a <span id="S1.F1.47" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;">calibration set</span> is available, we train another <span id="S1.F1.48" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;">MLP</span> to learn the three <span id="S1.F1.49" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;">MARs</span> (<math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}},\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">MAR</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">C</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">MAR</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">+</mo></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">,</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">MAR</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">C</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D9958F;" mathcolor="#D9958F">−</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}},\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) on this set (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="V-B Architecture and Learning for Classification ‣ V UQ Network Architecture and Learning ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B</span></a>). For <span id="S1.F1.50" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">test data</span>, its MAP heads produce three estimates used in self-consistency verification to calibrate the base model’s softmax confidence to capture <span id="S1.F1.51" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00B0F0;">aleatoric uncertainty</span> (Section <a href="#S6.SS2.SSS2" title="VI-B2 Aleatoric Uncertainty in Classification ‣ VI-B Quantifying Aleatoric Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-B2</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Background and Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review key UQ methods and situate our proposed UQ framework within this context.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Single-Forward-Pass UQ Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, these methods quantify uncertainty using a single forward pass of a deterministic task model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, either by modifying the model internally or by appending a post-hoc external uncertainty estimator without modifying the model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>1 </span>Internal Methods</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Internal methods require modifying a base model’s architecture or training loss to produce uncertainty estimates. Traditional methods, such as quantile regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and heteroscedastic regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, train a new model from scratch with a specialized loss for regression. More recent internal methods include evidential approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, deterministic UQ (DUQ) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and distance-aware models like SNGP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. These techniques are usually tailored to a single task and often capture only aleatoric or epistemic uncertainty; when both are modeled, they rely on strong prior or distributional assumptions and may require additional posterior inference at test time, complicating integration with existing base models. In contrast, our UQ framework requires no prior or distributional assumptions, no extra posterior inference at test time, and efficiently captures both aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty for different tasks, all while integrating seamlessly with the base model without modifying its architecture or retraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>2 </span>External Methods</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">External methods operate post hoc by attaching a separate module to a base model to estimate uncertainty from its predictions and extracted features. Common techniques model the feature distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, often under a specific distributional assumption. While easy to apply, these methods are generally limited to classification and capture only abrupt epistemic uncertainty for OOD detection rather than providing fine grain epistemic estimates.
A unified external method, SQR-OC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, estimates aleatoric uncertainty in regression via simultaneous QR and epistemic uncertainty via one class classification independently. However, SQR treats the full target set globally, which can produce misaligned PIs for complex distributions, and OC, used as a one-class classifier relying on a linear feature-space assumption, functions solely as an OOD detector.
Our framework also acts externally but differs in several key respects: (i) it requires no explicit distributional assumptions and delivers fine-grained, reliable epistemic estimates for both in-Distribution (iD) and OOD data across regression and classification; (ii) in regression, it jointly quantifies aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty via the SPA, yielding PIs with improved coverage and an epistemic-score based interval calibration under complex distributions; and (iii) in classification, when calibration data are available, it applies split-point self-consistency verification to adjust softmax outputs, providing a novel calibration method superior to commonly used temperature scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Forward-Pass UQ Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, these methods require multiple forward passes at test time to estimate uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>Bayesian and Ensemble</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bayesian and ensemble methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> require multiple forward passes at test time, yielding high quality uncertainty estimates, but incurring substantial computational and memory overhead which limits their practical use. In contrast, our framework requires only a single forward pass yet achieves UQ quality comparable to that of ensemble methods, since the three MAR estimates act as an implicit ensemble under the self-consistency constraint.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>Post-hoc Augmentation</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Post hoc augmentation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> estimate aleatoric uncertainty via test-time data augmentation, requiring multiple forward passes over perturbed inputs, despite no model architectural change. In contrast, our framework delivers both aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty estimates in real time with a single forward pass through a dedicated UQ network.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conformal Prediction</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conformal prediction (CP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is a model agnostic, distribution free, post hoc method that provides theoretical coverage guarantees for any ML model. However, CP requires a high quality calibration set and does not distinguish aleatoric from epistemic uncertainty. By contrast, our framework designed for DL remains model agnostic within that domain, disentangles aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty, and calibrates PIs in regression without additional calibration data, although it does not provide formal coverage guarantees.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Formulation and Foundations</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formulate the problem statement and establish the foundational elements of our UQ framework, including the split point analysis and the self consistency constraint.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Formulation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a supervised dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is paired with a target <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A deployed DL model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by fixed parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and hereafter termed the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">base</span> model, has been trained on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the true mapping <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\colon\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\colon\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})\approx F(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})\approx F(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given an unseen test set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{D}}=\{\hat{\bm{x}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{|\hat{\mathcal{D}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{D}}=\{\hat{\bm{x}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{|\hat{\mathcal{D}}|}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\cap\hat{\mathcal{D}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\cap\hat{\mathcal{D}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>), our goal is to accurately estimate both the aleatoric uncertainty, which stems from the inherent data noise <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(\hat{\bm{x}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\hat{\bm{x}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the epistemic uncertainty, which arises from the model’s approximation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\hat{\bm{x}_{i}};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\hat{\bm{x}_{i}};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> of the true function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\hat{\bm{x}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\hat{\bm{x}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For regression under heteroscedastic conditions, the underlying data-generating process is inherently stochastic and can be formalized as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}=F(\bm{x})+\varepsilon(\bm{x})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}=F(\bm{x})+\varepsilon(\bm{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true deterministic function and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is input-dependent noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To establish a unified UQ framework applicable to both regression and classification tasks, we extend the heteroscedastic regression setting in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-A Problem Formulation ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to classification. For multi-class classification, from a probabilistic perspective, we interpret the softmax output<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            In binary classification, the labels naturally follow a Bernoulli distribution.</span></span></span>, which follows a multinomial distribution, as the expectation of a generalized Bernoulli distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{y};\bm{p})=\prod_{k=1}^{K}p_{k}^{y_{k}}(1-p_{k})^{1-y_{k}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{y};\bm{p})=\prod_{k=1}^{K}p_{k}^{y_{k}}(1-p_{k})^{1-y_{k}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}=(y_{1},y_{2},\cdots,y_{K})\in\{0,1\}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}=(y_{1},y_{2},\cdots,y_{K})\in\{0,1\}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one-hot encoded label vector, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}=(p_{1},p_{2},\cdots,p_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}=(p_{1},p_{2},\cdots,p_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the predicted Bernoulli probabilities for the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes. Given a softmax output vector, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{y}}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})=(\tilde{y}_{1},\tilde{y}_{2},\cdots,\tilde{y}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{y}}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})=(\tilde{y}_{1},\tilde{y}_{2},\cdots,\tilde{y}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>,
we view each element <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{k}\in\tilde{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{k}\in\tilde{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the expected value of a class-specific Bernoulli distribution, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> signifies the probability that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to class <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\tilde{y}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\tilde{y}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to any class other than <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Leveraging this interpretation, we formulate heteroscedastic classification as a per-class problem for a given input <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{k}=F_{k}(\bm{x})+\varepsilon_{k}(\bm{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{k}=F_{k}(\bm{x})+\varepsilon_{k}(\bm{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(\bm{x})\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(\bm{x})\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true binary indicator for class <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{k}(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{k}(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> captures the input-dependent noise specifically associated with class <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this formulation, the softmax score for each class, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, naturally serves as the expected value of the noisy indicator <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Split-point Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in Section <a href="#S1" title="I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">split-point analysis</em> (SPA) underpins our UQ framework. We apply SPA separately to regression (continuous targets) and classification (discrete labels), and adopt element-wise notation throughout in the rest of this section for clarity and consistency.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>SPA for Regression</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each pair <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, let the base model prediction be <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> and define the residual <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=y-\tilde{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=y-\tilde{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. We collect the set of input-residual pairs where the residual is non-zero:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}=\{(\bm{x},r)\mid r\neq 0,(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=\{(\bm{x},r)\mid r\neq 0,(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This set is then partitioned based on the sign of the residual into a set of positive residuals:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{+}=\{(\bm{x},r)\mid r&gt;0,(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{+}=\{(\bm{x},r)\mid r&gt;0,(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and a set of negative residuals:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{-}=\{(\bm{x},r)\mid r&lt;0,(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{-}=\{(\bm{x},r)\mid r&lt;0,(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> captures underestimation errors while <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> captures overestimation errors. Residuals with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math> are omitted, since they occur with negligible probability and have minimal effect on uncertainty estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above SPA, we derive the total, upper side and lower side <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mean absolute residuals</em> (MARs) for any prediction <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}</annotation></semantics></math> on input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r)\in\mathcal{R},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r)\in\mathcal{R},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r)\in\mathcal{R}^{+},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r)\in\mathcal{R}^{+},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r)\in\mathcal{R}^{-},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r)\in\mathcal{R}^{-},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">These respectively measure the average magnitude of all residuals, underestimations, and overestimations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>SPA for Classification</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In classification, labels are discrete. For each class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the base model’s softmax probability <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{k}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{k}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and the one-hot label <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding a residual <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}=y_{k}-\tilde{y}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}=y_{k}-\tilde{y}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, we form <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{k}=\{(\bm{x},r_{k})\mid r_{k}\neq 0,(\bm{x},y_{k})\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{k}=\{(\bm{x},r_{k})\mid r_{k}\neq 0,(\bm{x},y_{k})\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{+}_{k}=\{(\bm{x},r_{k})\mid r_{k}&gt;0,(\bm{x},y_{k})\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{+}_{k}=\{(\bm{x},r_{k})\mid r_{k}&gt;0,(\bm{x},y_{k})\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{-}_{k}=\{(\bm{x},r_{k})\mid r_{k}&lt;0,(\bm{x},y_{k})\in\mathcal{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{-}_{k}=\{(\bm{x},r_{k})\mid r_{k}&lt;0,(\bm{x},y_{k})\in\mathcal{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Based on the heteroscedastic classification formulation in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-A Problem Formulation ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we derive the total, upper-side and lower-side MARs for any prediction <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> on input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E4.x1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.x1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.x1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\mathcal{R}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\mathcal{R}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E4.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P_{k}(\bm{x})(1-\tilde{y}_{k})+(1-P_{k}(\bm{x}))\tilde{y}_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P_{k}(\bm{x})(1-\tilde{y}_{k})+(1-P_{k}(\bm{x}))\tilde{y}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E4.x2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.x2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.x2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\mathcal{R}^{+}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\mathcal{R}^{+}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E4.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\tilde{y}_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\tilde{y}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E4.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\mathcal{R}^{-}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]=\tilde{y}_{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\mathcal{R}^{-}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\bigr]=\tilde{y}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the conditional class-frequency of class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> estimated from the training data in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notably, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the training data distribution, whereas <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> are derived directly from the base model’s softmax output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When a calibration dataset <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}~(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\neq\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}~(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\neq\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math> is available, we can use it to calibrate the initial UQ. To capture both the <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">magnitude</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frequency</em> of under- or over-prediction made by the base model, we define residuals for class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for any data point in
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}=y_{{\mathrm{C}},k}-\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}=y_{{\mathrm{C}},k}-\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{+}_{k}=\max\{r_{k},0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{+}_{k}=\max\{r_{k},0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{-}_{k}=\min\{r_{k},0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{-}_{k}=\min\{r_{k},0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Based on the zero-included residuals, we form
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{C}_{k}=\bigl\{(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},r_{k})|(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},y_{{\mathrm{C}},k})\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{C}_{k}=\bigl\{(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},r_{k})|(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},y_{{\mathrm{C}},k})\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{C}^{+}_{k}=\bigl\{(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},r_{k}^{+})|(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},y_{{\mathrm{C}},k})\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{C}^{+}_{k}=\bigl\{(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},r_{k}^{+})|(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},y_{{\mathrm{C}},k})\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{C}^{-}_{k}=\bigl\{(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},r_{k}^{-})|(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},y_{{\mathrm{C}},k})\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{C}^{-}_{k}=\bigl\{(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},r_{k}^{-})|(\bm{x}_{\mathrm{C}},y_{{\mathrm{C}},k})\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we derive the total, upper-side, and lower-side <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zero-included MARs</span> for any prediction <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}</annotation></semantics></math> on input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3a"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3a.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\operatorname{C}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\operatorname{C}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x})(1-\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k})+(1-P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x}))\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x})(1-\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k})+(1-P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x}))\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\operatorname{C}^{+}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\operatorname{C}^{+}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x})\bigl(1-\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x})\bigl(1-\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\operatorname{C}^{-}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[|r_{k}|\mid(\bm{x^{\prime}},r_{k})\in\operatorname{C}^{-}_{k},\bm{x^{\prime}}=\bm{x}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl(1-P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x})\bigr)\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl(1-P_{{\mathrm{C}},k}(\bm{x})\bigr)\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C,k}(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C,k}(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is the frequency of class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and estimated from the calibration set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast to (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) employs these statistical estimates to weight each residual by its empirical likelihood, resulting in adjustments that vanish in well-calibrated regions and grow only where both error magnitude and occurrence frequency are high. The derivations of MARs in
(<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) are provided in Appendix A.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe a general relationship among split-point statistical quantities that holds under any distribution over a finite set, and refer to this property as <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">self-consistency constraint</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Self-Consistency Constraint)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a real-valued random variable with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbb{E}[Y]|&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbb{E}[Y]|&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. For a split-point <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the total Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD), upper-side <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and lower-side
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAD}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[\lvert Y-t\rvert\mid Y\neq t\bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[\lvert Y-t\rvert\mid Y\neq t\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAD}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAD}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[Y-t\mid Y&gt;t\bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[Y-t\mid Y&gt;t\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAD}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAD}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[t-Y\mid Y&lt;t\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\bigl[t-Y\mid Y&lt;t\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\mathbb{E}[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\mathbb{E}[Y]</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y&gt;t)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y&gt;t)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y&lt;t)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y&lt;t)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the following identity holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}=H\bigl(\mathrm{MAD}^{+},\,\mathrm{MAD}^{-}\bigr)=\frac{2\,\mathrm{MAD}^{+}\,\mathrm{MAD}^{-}}{\mathrm{MAD}^{+}+\mathrm{MAD}^{-}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>MAD</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}=H\bigl(\mathrm{MAD}^{+},\,\mathrm{MAD}^{-}\bigr)=\frac{2\,\mathrm{MAD}^{+}\,\mathrm{MAD}^{-}}{\mathrm{MAD}^{+}+\mathrm{MAD}^{-}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(a,b)=2ab/(a+b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(a,b)=2ab/(a+b)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the harmonic mean.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ A-B Proof of Theorem (Self-Consistency Constraint) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> implies the following proposition:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Minimum Discrepancy)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y&gt;t)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y&gt;t)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y&lt;t)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y&lt;t)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define the self-consistency discrepancy:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(t):=\left|\mathrm{MAD}-H\bigl(\mathrm{MAD}^{+},\,\mathrm{MAD}^{-}\bigr)\right|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(t):=\left|\mathrm{MAD}-H\bigl(\mathrm{MAD}^{+},\,\mathrm{MAD}^{-}\bigr)\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(t)</annotation></semantics></math> attains its global minimum of zero when <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\mathbb{E}[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\mathbb{E}[Y]</annotation></semantics></math> and at any balance points where <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{+}=\mathrm{MAD}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{+}=\mathrm{MAD}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proofs of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ A-B Proof of Theorem (Self-Consistency Constraint) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (Minimum Discrepancy). ‣ A-C Proof of Proposition (Minimum Discrepancy) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are provided in Appendix A.
Under the self-consistency constraint of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ A-B Proof of Theorem (Self-Consistency Constraint) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (Minimum Discrepancy). ‣ A-C Proof of Proposition (Minimum Discrepancy) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> suggests that the discrepancy
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\tilde{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\tilde{t})</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the deviation of any estimate <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> from the true mean <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y]</annotation></semantics></math>.
If predictive bias is interpreted as epistemic uncertainty and the MAD components can be estimated, then <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\tilde{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\tilde{t})</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a natural metric for quantifying this uncertainty.
Moreover, since (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Theorem 1 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is homogeneous in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAD</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\tilde{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\tilde{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under their uniform scaling. Thus, aleatoric noise, which merely rescales all deviations, leaves <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\tilde{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\tilde{t})</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged, whereas epistemic bias, by skewing <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAD}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAD}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, alters it, enabling separation of epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty in UQ.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the zero-included MARs on calibration data, we have the<span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> zero-included self-consistency constraint</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Calibration Identity)<span id="Thmproposition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}</annotation></semantics></math> be a calibration set, where <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\neq\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}\neq\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the zero<span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>included MARs from (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{MAR}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})+\mathrm{MAR}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{MAR}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})+\mathrm{MAR}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{\mathrm{C},k}=\tilde{y}_{\mathrm{C},k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{\mathrm{C},k}=\tilde{y}_{\mathrm{C},k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathrm{MAR}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})-\mathrm{MAR}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathrm{MAR}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x})-\mathrm{MAR}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},k}|\bm{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (Calibration Identity). ‣ A-D Proof of Proposition (Calibration Identity) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is provided in Appendix A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">According to statistical decision theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, Section 2.4]</cite>, the optimal prediction under the mean squared loss is the conditional mean <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
For unbiased model prediction <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}=\mathbb{E}[Y|X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}=\mathbb{E}[Y|X]</annotation></semantics></math>, the MAR coincides with the MAD. In this case, for any prediction <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, the observed MARs in a sample serve as empirical estimates of the conditional-level MADs:
<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MAR}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})=\operatorname{MAD}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x}),~\operatorname{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})=\operatorname{MAD}^{+}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>MAD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MAR}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})=\operatorname{MAD}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x}),~\operatorname{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})=\operatorname{MAD}^{+}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})=\operatorname{MAD}^{-}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MAD</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})=\operatorname{MAD}^{-}(\tilde{t}|\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the theoretical results in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ A-B Proof of Theorem (Self-Consistency Constraint) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (Minimum Discrepancy). ‣ A-C Proof of Proposition (Minimum Discrepancy) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> apply directly to conditional MARs and serve as the theoretical grounding for our UQ framework based on split-point self-consistency verification. Moreover, when a calibration set is available, we apply the self-consistency identities in Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4 (Calibration Identity). ‣ A-D Proof of Proposition (Calibration Identity) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to recalibrate softmax outputs, enhancing the reliability of aleatoric uncertainty estimates in classification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UQ Network Architecture and Learning</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our UQ network architecture for learning the quantities required for UQ through split-point self-consistency verification, as detailed in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, along with its training procedure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, working in a post-hoc
manner<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          If a base model does not exist, our method allows training the base model and the UQ network stagewise or jointly.</span></span></span>, our UQ network builds upon an established base model,
<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})=g\bigl(h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})=g\bigl(h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the last hidden layer features or the penultimate layer output, and <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output layer’s transfer activation. Hereafter, we denote <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}=h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}=h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> as the feature map of <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted by <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notably, for any input <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, our UQ network requires only the feature map <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and the base model output <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{y}}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{y}}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> to perform the SPA described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architecture and Learning for Regression</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on a training dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            The dataset may differ from the base model’s training set, provided it follows the same distribution. Here, we present the method for univariate regression; its extension to multivariate regression is straightforward.</span></span></span>, our UQ network operates on a regression base model to perform SPA-based quantile regression for aleatoric UQ and to estimate three MARs described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B1</span></a> for epistemic UQ.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the left plot of Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we employ a fully connected MLP regressor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\bm{h};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\bm{h};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math>
with parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, as our UQ network. We train <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\bm{h};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\bm{h};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> to learn</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{QR}^{+}\times\mathcal{QR}^{-}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{QR}^{+}\times\mathcal{QR}^{-}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{QR}^{+},~\mathcal{QR}^{-},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{QR}^{+},~\mathcal{QR}^{-},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
are, respectively, the spaces of upper-side residuals, lower-side residuals, and the total, upper-side, and lower-side MARs.
Hence, its output is
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi\bigr)=\bigl({q}^{+},{q}^{-},{z},{z}^{+},{z}^{-}\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi\bigr)=\bigl({q}^{+},{q}^{-},{z},{z}^{+},{z}^{-}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math>
Here, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
are two independent quantile regression (QR) heads corresponding to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
estimate the total, upper-side, and lower-side MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We construct two training sets for the split-point quantile regression:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{+}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{+}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{+}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{+}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},-r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{-}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{-}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},-r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{-}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{-}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Based on these datasets, we train two QR heads to learn
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\tau^{+}},\,Q_{\tau^{-}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathbb{R}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\tau^{+}},\,Q_{\tau^{-}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\tau^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\tau^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> fitted on <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\tau^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\tau^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math> fitted on <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{+},\tau^{-}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{+},\tau^{-}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the marginal confidence levels for the upper and lower quantiles, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For notational simplicity, we drop the explicit conditioning on <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and write
<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
as the shorthand <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Furthermore, we construct the training set for estimating the three MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Using these datasets, we train three MAR heads to learn
<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{MAR}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{MAR}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
During training, we use the calibration-aware loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>,
<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{QR}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>QR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{QR}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, for quantile regression, and the mean square error (MSE) loss <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> for MAR prediction. Hence, the optimal shared parameters are obtained by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\Phi}\Bigl[\,L_{\mathrm{QR}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>QR</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\Phi}\Bigl[\,L_{\mathrm{QR}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad+L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi\bigr)\Bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad+L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi\bigr)\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architecture and Learning for Classification</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on a training dataset <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, our UQ network operates on a classification base model to estimate only the total MAR described in (<a href="#S3.E4.1" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a>), as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E4.2" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4b</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E4.3" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4c</span></a>) are derived directly from the base model’s softmax output. As shown in the right plot of Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we employ a fully connected MLP regressor <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{T}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{T}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> to learn</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{T}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{T}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of total MAR.
Hence, its output is the estimated total MAR:
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{T}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)=\bm{z}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{T}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)=\bm{z},</annotation></semantics></math>
the estimated <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}</annotation></semantics></math> across the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes for any input <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to the base model via its feature map <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We construct the training set for estimating the total MAR defined in (<a href="#S3.E4.1" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a>):
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shorthand <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We employ the MSE loss <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> for learning total MAR prediction. Therefore, the optimal parameters are obtained by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{T}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{T}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When a calibration dataset <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is available, we learn to estimate the three MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) for calibration. As shown in the right plot of Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we employ another fully connected MLP regressor <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{C}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{C}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> to learn</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{C}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{C}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
are the spaces of total, upper-side, and lower-side zero-included MARs.
Hence, its output is
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{C}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)=\bigl({\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{C}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)=\bigl({\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math>
Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
estimate the total, upper-side, and lower-side zero-included MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) for all <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes.
We construct the training set for estimating these zero-included MARs:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are the shorthand
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use the MSE loss
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for learning the prediction of three MARs used in calibration. Hence, the optimal shared parameters are obtained by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{C}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{C}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All the loss function definitions, the pseudo-code of the learning algorithms and a computational complexity analysis are provided in Appendix B.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">UQ Network Architecture and Learning</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our UQ network architecture for learning the quantities required for UQ through split-point self-consistency verification, as detailed in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, along with its training procedure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, working in a post-hoc
manner<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          If a base model does not exist, our method allows training the base model and the UQ network stagewise or jointly.</span></span></span>, our UQ network builds upon an established base model,
<math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})=g\bigl(h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})=g\bigl(h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the last hidden layer features or the penultimate layer output, and <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the output layer’s transfer activation. Hereafter, we denote <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}=h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}=h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> as the feature map of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted by <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notably, for any input <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, our UQ network requires only the feature map <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and the base model output <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{y}}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{y}}=f(\bm{x};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> to perform the SPA described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architecture and Learning for Regression</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on a training dataset <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            The dataset may differ from the base model’s training set, provided it follows the same distribution. Here, we present the method for univariate regression; its extension to multivariate regression is straightforward.</span></span></span>, our UQ network operates on a regression base model to perform SPA-based quantile regression for aleatoric UQ and to estimate three MARs described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B1</span></a> for epistemic UQ.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the left plot of Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we employ a fully connected MLP regressor <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\bm{h};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\bm{h};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math>
with parameters <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, as our UQ network. We train <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\bm{h};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\bm{h};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> to learn</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{QR}^{+}\times\mathcal{QR}^{-}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{QR}^{+}\times\mathcal{QR}^{-}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{QR}^{+},~\mathcal{QR}^{-},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{QR}^{+},~\mathcal{QR}^{-},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
are, respectively, the spaces of upper-side residuals, lower-side residuals, and the total, upper-side, and lower-side MARs.
Hence, its output is
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi\bigr)=\bigl({q}^{+},{q}^{-},{z},{z}^{+},{z}^{-}\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi\bigr)=\bigl({q}^{+},{q}^{-},{z},{z}^{+},{z}^{-}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math>
Here, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
are two independent quantile regression (QR) heads corresponding to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
estimate the total, upper-side, and lower-side MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We construct two training sets for the split-point quantile regression:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{+}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{+}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{+}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{+}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},-r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{-}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{-}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},-r_{i}\bigr)|r_{i}\in\mathcal{R}^{-}\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{R}^{-}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Based on these datasets, we train two QR heads to learn
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\tau^{+}},\,Q_{\tau^{-}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathbb{R}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\tau^{+}},\,Q_{\tau^{-}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\tau^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\tau^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> fitted on <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\tau^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\tau^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math> fitted on <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{+},\tau^{-}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{+},\tau^{-}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the marginal confidence levels for the upper and lower quantiles, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For notational simplicity, we drop the explicit conditioning on <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and write
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{i}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>
as the shorthand <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Furthermore, we construct the training set for estimating the three MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III-B1 SPA for Regression ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S5.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{+}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},\mathrm{MAR}_{i}^{-}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Using these datasets, we train three MAR heads to learn
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{MAR}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{MAR}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
During training, we use the calibration-aware loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>,
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{QR}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>QR</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{QR}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, for quantile regression, and the mean square error (MSE) loss <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> for MAR prediction. Hence, the optimal shared parameters are obtained by</p>
<table id="S5.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\Phi}\Bigl[\,L_{\mathrm{QR}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>QR</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>QR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\min_{\Phi}\Bigl[\,L_{\mathrm{QR}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{QR}}^{-},\tau^{+},\tau^{-};\Phi\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad+L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi\bigr)\Bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad+L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{+},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}^{-};\Phi\bigr)\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architecture and Learning for Classification</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on a training dataset <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{i},\bm{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, our UQ network operates on a classification base model to estimate only the total MAR described in (<a href="#S3.E4.1" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a>), as <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E4.2" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4b</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E4.3" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4c</span></a>) are derived directly from the base model’s softmax output. As shown in the right plot of Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we employ a fully connected MLP regressor <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{T}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{T}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> to learn</p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{T}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{T}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\mathrm{MAR}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of total MAR.
Hence, its output is the estimated total MAR:
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{T}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)=\bm{z}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{T}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)=\bm{z},</annotation></semantics></math>
the estimated <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MAR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}</annotation></semantics></math> across the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes for any input <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to the base model via its feature map <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We construct the training set for estimating the total MAR defined in (<a href="#S3.E4.1" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4a</span></a>):
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}}=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shorthand <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We employ the MSE loss <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> for learning total MAR prediction. Therefore, the optimal parameters are obtained by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{T}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>MAR</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{T}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}\bigl(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}\bigr)\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When a calibration dataset <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}=\{(\bm{x}_{{\mathrm{C}},i},\bm{y}_{{\mathrm{C}},i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is available, we learn to estimate the three MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) for calibration. As shown in the right plot of Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we employ another fully connected MLP regressor <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{C}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{C}}(\bm{h},\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> to learn</p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\mathrm{C}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\mathrm{C}}\colon h(\mathcal{X})\to\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}\times\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},~\mathcal{Z}_{{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
are the spaces of total, upper-side, and lower-side zero-included MARs.
Hence, its output is
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{C}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)=\bigl({\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{C}}\bigl(\bm{h};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)=\bigl({\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math>
Here, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
estimate the total, upper-side, and lower-side zero-included MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) for all <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes.
We construct the training set for estimating these zero-included MARs:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\{\bigl(\bm{h}_{\mathrm{C},i},(\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-})_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)\bigr\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{C}}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{+},\mathrm{MAR}_{{\mathrm{C}},ik}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are the shorthand
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i}),\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}(\tilde{y}_{ik}|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use the MSE loss
<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for learning the prediction of three MARs used in calibration. Hence, the optimal shared parameters are obtained by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{C}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>MSE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msubsup><mi>MAR</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}_{\mathrm{C}}=\arg\min_{\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}}\Bigl[L_{\mathrm{MSE}}(\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}},\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{MAR}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}})\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All the loss function definitions, the pseudo-code of the learning algorithms and a computational complexity analysis are provided in Appendix B.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce our UQ method, guided by the self-consistency principle (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-C</span></a>) and supported by trained UQ networks (Section <a href="#S5" title="V UQ Network Architecture and Learning ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig.  <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for each test point <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}\in\hat{\mathcal{D}}=\{\hat{\bm{x}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{|\hat{\mathcal{D}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}\in\hat{\mathcal{D}}=\{\hat{\bm{x}}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{|\hat{\mathcal{D}}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pretrained base model and the trained UQ networks supply all quantities required by our UQ method. Specifically, the base model produces the feature map <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{h}}=h(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{h}}=h(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> and prediction <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}=f(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}=f(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. In regression, the UQ network <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> outputs <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{q}^{+},\hat{q}^{-},\hat{z},\hat{z}^{+},\hat{z}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{q}^{+},\hat{q}^{-},\hat{z},\hat{z}^{+},\hat{z}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>. In classification, <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{T}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{T}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quantifying Epistemic Uncertainty</span></h3>

<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>1 </span>Self-Consistency Discrepancy Score</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify epistemic uncertainty, we adapt Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (Minimum Discrepancy). ‣ A-C Proof of Proposition (Minimum Discrepancy) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to define the <em id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-consistency discrepancy score</em> (SDS):</p>
<table id="S6.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}=\bigl|2\,\mathrm{MAR}^{+}\,\mathrm{MAR}^{-}-\mathrm{MAR}\,(\mathrm{MAR}^{+}+\mathrm{MAR}^{-})\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>MAR</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>MAR</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}=\bigl|2\,\mathrm{MAR}^{+}\,\mathrm{MAR}^{-}-\mathrm{MAR}\,(\mathrm{MAR}^{+}+\mathrm{MAR}^{-})\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This formulation avoids the division in the harmonic mean in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Theorem 1 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), thereby reducing numerical instability and extreme values. It preserves the core self-consistency discrepancy and yields more robust estimates of epistemic uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The SDS at a test point <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects two factors: (i) the bias of the base model’s prediction <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the true conditional expectation <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\big[\bm{y}|\hat{\bm{x}}\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\big[\bm{y}|\hat{\bm{x}}\big]</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) the model’s lack of knowledge in the neighbourhood of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which induces stochastic deviations that break the consistency among MAR estimates. Thus, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SDS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>SDS</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SDS}</annotation></semantics></math> captures both predictive bias and distributional mismatch, enabling fine<span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>grained quantification of epistemic uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In regression, the UQ network <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> provides the three MAR estimates <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to (<a href="#S6.E8" title="In VI-A1 Self-Consistency Discrepancy Score ‣ VI-A Quantifying Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), for a test prediction <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the SDS is simply</p>
<table id="S6.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{y}|\hat{\bm{x}})=\bigl|2\hat{z}^{+}\hat{z}^{-}-\hat{z}(\hat{z}^{+}+\hat{z}^{-})\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{y}|\hat{\bm{x}})=\bigl|2\hat{z}^{+}\hat{z}^{-}-\hat{z}(\hat{z}^{+}+\hat{z}^{-})\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In classification, we directly obtain the upper- and lower-side MARs defined in (<a href="#S3.E4.2" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4b</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E4.3" title="In 4 ‣ III-B2 SPA for Classification ‣ III-B Split-point Analysis ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4c</span></a>) from the base model, as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{z}}^{+}=\bm{1}-\hat{\bm{y}},\quad\hat{\bm{z}}^{-}=\hat{\bm{y}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{z}}^{+}=\bm{1}-\hat{\bm{y}},\quad\hat{\bm{z}}^{-}=\hat{\bm{y}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the all-ones vector.
Together with the total MAR <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> estimated by the UQ network <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{T}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{T}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{T}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, according to (<a href="#S6.E8" title="In VI-A1 Self-Consistency Discrepancy Score ‣ VI-A Quantifying Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), for a test prediction <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the SDS becomes</p>
<table id="S6.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})=\lVert 2\hat{\bm{z}}^{+}\odot\hat{\bm{z}}^{-}-\hat{\bm{z}}\odot(\hat{\bm{z}}^{+}+\hat{\bm{z}}^{-})\bigr\rVert_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})=\lVert 2\hat{\bm{z}}^{+}\odot\hat{\bm{z}}^{-}-\hat{\bm{z}}\odot(\hat{\bm{z}}^{+}+\hat{\bm{z}}^{-})\bigr\rVert_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\cdot\rVert_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\cdot\rVert_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{\textbullet}{l}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>•</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{\textbullet}{l}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> is the operator for element-wise multiplication.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span>2 </span>Out-of-Distribution Detection</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SDS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>SDS</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SDS}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-grained</span> metric for quantifying epistemic uncertainty on iD data, it can also detect OOD points and other high-uncertainty cases. In practice, we first compute <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SDS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>SDS</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SDS}</annotation></semantics></math> values on a held<span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>out iD validation set to build a reference distribution (e.g., via histogram) and choose a threshold <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as its upper <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>quantile (e.g., 95th percentile). At inference, for any test input <math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and flag the point as OOD if
<math id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})&gt;\Delta^{\prime}_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})&gt;\Delta^{\prime}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quantifying Aleatoric Uncertainty</span></h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>1 </span>Aleatoric Uncertainty in Regression</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The UQ network for regression, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{h}}=h(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{h}}=h(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, has two QR heads that produce the estimates <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to marginal confidence levels <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a test point <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}\in\hat{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}\in\hat{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> with prediction <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=f(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=f(\hat{\bm{x}};\Theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <em id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">split-point prediction interval</em> (SPI) as</p>
<table id="S6.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\;\in\;\bigl[\hat{y}-\hat{q}^{-},\;\hat{y}+\hat{q}^{+}\bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\;\in\;\bigl[\hat{y}-\hat{q}^{-},\;\hat{y}+\hat{q}^{+}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By separating over- and under-estimation residuals and constructing bounds for each side, the SPI yields a more informative quantification of aleatoric uncertainty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, the estimates <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> may be noisy, especially when <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{y}|\hat{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\prime}(\hat{y}|\hat{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S6.E9" title="In VI-A1 Self-Consistency Discrepancy Score ‣ VI-A Quantifying Epistemic Uncertainty ‣ VI Uncertainty Quantification via Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is large, rendering the SPI unreliable. To improve robustness, we enforce the self-consistency constraint from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ A-B Proof of Theorem (Self-Consistency Constraint) ‣ Appendix A Derivation and Proofs ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using the shared UQ network of the two QR heads and three MAR heads. From (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Theorem 1 (Self-Consistency Constraint). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the MAR outputs must satisfy the harmonic relation, which implies</p>
<table id="S6.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\hat{z}\,\hat{z}^{-}}{2\,\hat{z}^{-}-\hat{z}},\quad\hat{z}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\hat{z}\,\hat{z}^{+}}{2\,\hat{z}^{+}-\hat{z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\hat{z}\,\hat{z}^{-}}{2\,\hat{z}^{-}-\hat{z}},\quad\hat{z}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\hat{z}\,\hat{z}^{+}}{2\,\hat{z}^{+}-\hat{z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To enhance reliability in regions prone to under-coverage, we define the <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">calibration factors</span> as follows:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\max\bigl(\hat{z}^{+},\,\hat{z}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)}{\hat{z}^{+}},\quad s^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\max\bigl(\hat{z}^{-},\,\hat{z}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)}{\hat{z}^{-}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\max\bigl(\hat{z}^{+},\,\hat{z}^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)}{\hat{z}^{+}},\quad s^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{\max\bigl(\hat{z}^{-},\,\hat{z}^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}\bigr)}{\hat{z}^{-}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Applying these scaling factors yields the <em id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">calibrated</em> SPI:</p>
<table id="S6.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\;\in\;\bigl[\hat{y}-s^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}\,\hat{q}^{-},\;\hat{y}+s^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}\,\hat{q}^{+}\bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\;\in\;\bigl[\hat{y}-s^{-}_{\mathrm{C}}\,\hat{q}^{-},\;\hat{y}+s^{+}_{\mathrm{C}}\,\hat{q}^{+}\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span>2 </span>Aleatoric Uncertainty in Classification</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a test input <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with softmax output <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, its aleatoric uncertainty is quantified via the predictive entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S6.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Ent(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})=-\sum_{k=1}^{K}\hat{y}_{k}\,\log\bigl(\hat{y}_{k}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ent</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ent(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})=-\sum_{k=1}^{K}\hat{y}_{k}\,\log\bigl(\hat{y}_{k}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When calibration data are available, we utilize the zero-included MAR self-consistency encoded in
(<a href="#S3.E7.1" title="In 7 ‣ Proposition 2 (Calibration Identity). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7a</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E7.2" title="In 7 ‣ Proposition 2 (Calibration Identity). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7b</span></a>)
for calibrating a base model’s prediction.
Based on the vectorial form of (<a href="#S3.E7.1" title="In 7 ‣ Proposition 2 (Calibration Identity). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7a</span></a>) and the UQ network output
<math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}^{*})=\bigl(\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{h}};\Phi_{\mathrm{C}}^{*})=\bigl(\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+},\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">calibration quality-assurance factor</span> for a test point <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with prediction <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})=\lVert\,\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}-\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}-\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\,\rVert_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})=\lVert\,\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}-\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}-\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}\,\rVert_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A small <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> indicates reliable calibration, so we adjust <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> only if <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})&lt;\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})&lt;\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen via cross-validation. Applying the vectorial form of (<a href="#S3.E7.2" title="In 7 ‣ Proposition 2 (Calibration Identity). ‣ III-C Split-Point Self-Consistency Principle ‣ III Problem Formulation and Foundations ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7b</span></a>), the calibrated prediction <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}_{\mathrm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}_{\mathrm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes</p>
<table id="S6.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{y}}_{\mathrm{C}}=\hat{\bm{y}}+\mathbbm{1}\bigl(\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})&lt;\delta_{0}\bigr)(\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}-\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{y}}_{\mathrm{C}}=\hat{\bm{y}}+\mathbbm{1}\bigl(\delta_{\mathrm{C}}(\hat{\bm{y}}|\hat{\bm{x}})&lt;\delta_{0}\bigr)(\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{+}-\hat{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{C}}^{-}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.
In practice, we find that setting <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> yields stable and reliable results across all experimental datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe our experimental setup, report results for the regression and classification tasks, respectively, and summarize extended experimental findings in the supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span> </span><span id="S7.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we outline our experimental setup, and all the details are provided in Appendix C to ensure reproducibility.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>1 </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For regression, we first consider a synthetic cubic regression task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x^{3}+\epsilon(x)-\mathbb{E}[\epsilon(x)],\quad\epsilon(x)\sim\mathrm{LogNormal}(1.5,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>LogNormal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x^{3}+\epsilon(x)-\mathbb{E}[\epsilon(x)],\quad\epsilon(x)\sim\mathrm{LogNormal}(1.5,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which enables an illustrative study of asymmetric noise. Next, we use nine UCI regression benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, widely used for UQ evaluation.
Finally, we evaluate on a high-dimensional monocular depth estimation dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> to assess performance in complex real-world scenarios. To test fine-grained uncertainty estimation, we generate adversarial variants using the Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, where the perturbation magnitude is controlled by a parameter <math id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For classification, we use CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, and ImageNet-1K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> as iD datasets, augmenting them with FGSM adversarial variants. For OOD detection, we evaluate on SVHN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, Tiny ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, and ImageNet-O/A <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Finally, we assess performance in real-world multimodal scenarios using the LUMA benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13262v1/unc-decomposition.png" id="S7.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/128;" width="476" height="128" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Uncertainties quantified for the cubic regression using (a) Deep Ensemble (DE), (b) Evidential Regression (EDL-R), (c) SQR-OC, (d) our method without calibration, and (e) our method with calibration. <span id="S7.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top row</span> shows aleatoric uncertainty estimates, <span id="S7.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bottom row</span> shows epistemic uncertainty estimates. Ground truth and true PI boundaries are shown as dashed lines.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>2 </span>Baselines and Base Models</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our comparative study, we adopt several state-of-the-art baselines within single-forward-pass, including internal and external methods, and multi-forward-pass categories (see Section <a href="#S2" title="II Background and Related Work ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>):
(i) Bayesian-based methods, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MC-Dropout</span> (MD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Laplace Approximation</span> (LA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>;
(ii) ensemble-based method, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deep Ensemble</span> (DE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>;
(iii) internal (evidential) methods, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evidential Regression</span> (EDL-R) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evidential Quantile Regression</span> (EDL-QR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evidential Classification</span> (EDL-C) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>;
(iv) external methods, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SQR-OC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DDU</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As reviewed in Section <a href="#S2" title="II Background and Related Work ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, external methods operate on a base model. For regression, we train fully connected MLPs as base models on the synthetic cubic regression task and the nine UCI benchmarks. For monocular depth estimation, we train a U-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> as the base model. In image classification, we employ two CNN architectures, VGG-16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> and Wide ResNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, as base models. For CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, both networks are trained from scratch, whereas for ImageNet-1K we use pretrained models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. Finally, as the base model for the multimodal task, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, we train a CNN to encode the visual modality and Transformer encoders to process the audio and text modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>3 </span>Experimental Protocol</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate all models under identical settings, including the same training, validation and test splits, and a consistent hyperparameter search. For regression, MD and DE use Gaussian-likelihood regression, while other methods rely on their own evidential or quantile-based distributions to construct 95% prediction intervals (PIs). Point predictions are defined as the predictive mean in Gaussian-based models, the 50th percentile in quantile regression, and the MSE-optimal output in our framework. For classification, methods without a built-in aleatoric calibration mechanism employ Temperature Scaling (TS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. For hyperparameter tuning, we randomly reserve 10% of the training data as a validation set and perform <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold cross validation, with <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=20</annotation></semantics></math> for synthetic, UCI datasets and CIFAR-10/CIFAR-100, and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> for the remaining datasets due to computational constraints. To simulate calibration data, we further sample 10% of the training set without data leakage. All models are evaluated on the predefined test sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For our method, we also conduct extended experiments to assess joint training of the base model and UQ network from scratch<sup id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><a href="#footnote4" title="footnote 4 ‣ V UQ Network Architecture and Learning ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></sup>, evaluate robustness to training data volume<sup id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><a href="#footnote5" title="footnote 5 ‣ V-A Architecture and Learning for Regression ‣ V UQ Network Architecture and Learning ‣ Post-Hoc Split-Point Self-Consistency Verification for Efficient, Unified Quantification of Aleatoric and Epistemic Uncertainty in Deep Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></sup>, and test our confidence calibration under the same settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>4 </span>Evaluation Criteria</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess UQ performance using the following criteria:
(i) <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span>, measured by <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">root mean squared error</span> (RMSE) for regression point predictions and by prediction accuracy for classification;
(ii) <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aleatoric uncertainty</span>, quantified by <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">expected calibration error</span> (ECE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> for classification, and by <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prediction interval expected calibration error</span> (PIECE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and Winkler score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> for regression.
Motivated by our SPA, we also adopt fine grained split-point metrics <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PIECE}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>PIECE</mtext><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PIECE}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PIECE}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>PIECE</mtext><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PIECE}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> on the upper and lower split point intervals, respectively. This decomposition measures overestimation and underestimation separately and applies to any model yielding point predictions;
(iii) <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Epistemic uncertainty</span>, evaluated via the Spearman <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correlation</span> coefficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> for regression and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">area under the receiver operating characteristic curve</span> (AUROC) for classification, reflecting the separability of adversarial, OOD and error samples;
(iv) <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency</span>, assessed by training and inference time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>5 </span>Implementation</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement our framework in Python using PyTorch and its built-in Adam and SGD optimizers<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
              Our source code is available at <a href="https://github.com/zzz0527/SPC-UQ" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/zzz0527/SPC-UQ</a>.</span></span></span>. Experiments run on an NVIDIA A100 GPU with 16 GB of memory, while ImageNet-1K experiments use an NVIDIA V100 GPU with 80 GB of memory. For each baseline, we adapt the original authors’ open-source code on the same platform and retain their default hyperparameters.</p>
</div>
